$translations = array(
    '1957' => <<<'HTML'
<p>The story of <strong>1957</strong> begins in that very year, when Gregorio started out in the world of olive growing. Since then, olive oil has passed from one generation to the next, becoming a way of life now shared by <strong>three generations</strong> of the same family.</p><p>That connection with the olive grove is also the starting point for their oils. To produce their <strong>extra virgin olive oils</strong>, they work with olives picked directly from the tree at their optimum stage of ripeness, aiming to ensure that the fruit reaches the mill in the best possible condition.</p><p>From there begins a process in which every detail is designed to preserve the olive's natural qualities. After cleaning, the fruit is milled and the oil is obtained exclusively by mechanical means. Throughout the process it remains in contact with inert materials such as <strong>stainless steel</strong> to prevent any alteration.</p><p>One of the defining aspects of their production is temperature control. Their EVOOs are extracted <strong>cold</strong>, keeping the transformation process below <strong>19 °C</strong> in order to preserve as much as possible of the aromas, flavours and properties naturally present in the fruit.</p><p>The experience accumulated since 1957 has also been accompanied by various awards recognising the quality of their oils. These include first prizes in awards for the best EVOOs from the <strong>Guadalquivir Valley</strong>, in both intense green-fruit and ripe-fruit categories, as well as recognition of the career Gregorio began within the local olive-oil sector.</p><p>Today, 1957 maintains the same connection between family, olive grove and mill that gave rise to the project. Three generations later, they continue to make their oils by combining experience passed down over decades with particular care for the raw material and for every stage involved in its transformation.</p>
HTML,
    'hidalgo-de-la-jara' => <<<'HTML'
<p>The story of <strong>Hidalgo de la Jara</strong> is deeply connected to the Iberian pig and the dehesa of Los Pedroches. Five generations have been dedicated to breeding <strong>100% Iberian pigs</strong>, a breed they have known from the very beginning and around which they have developed their entire way of working.</p><p>Everything starts in the countryside. The pigs are born and raised free-range, first alongside their mothers and, once they can fend for themselves, making use of the resources they find in the dehesa. Piglets born in autumn may even enjoy their first acorns during weaning; later comes the <em>montanera</em>, when the animals destined for acorn finishing complete their final fattening period by feeding on acorns, grass and other natural resources from the surrounding land.</p><p>The dehesa remains part of their diet throughout the rest of the year. In summer, when its natural resources are scarcer, <strong>chickpeas, broad beans, vetch, oats and other cereals and pulses</strong> are sown for the pigs to graze <em>ad libitum</em>, feeding <strong>directly from the land</strong>. It is a way of keeping their farming closely connected to the countryside even outside the montanera season.</p><p>Over the years, that experience in breeding Iberian pigs led Hidalgo de la Jara to take responsibility for making their products as well. In <strong>Villanueva de Córdoba, in the heart of Los Pedroches</strong>, they control the process from raising the animals through to the production and maturation of their hams and shoulders.</p><p>That same connection with their surroundings is also reflected in their facilities, where they use a <strong>photovoltaic solar energy</strong> self-consumption system, bringing renewable energy into an activity that has always been closely linked to the resources and landscape of Los Pedroches.</p><p>After salting and settling under controlled conditions, the hams and shoulders move to natural cellars, where the local temperatures accompany their maturation slowly. Cold winters and hot summers form part of a gradual curing process that, for their hams, lasts for more than <strong>30 months</strong>, allowing each piece the time it needs to develop its aromas, texture and character.</p><p>Hidalgo de la Jara's connection with Los Pedroches goes beyond its location. They were among the promoters of the <strong>Los Pedroches Designation of Origin</strong>, working alongside other livestock farmers and producers in the region on the development of the criteria that would ultimately define the designation.</p><p>Five generations later, Hidalgo de la Jara continues to work with the Iberian pig from the same place where its entire journey begins: the dehesa. Their approach to livestock farming and production is one in which experience, the surrounding environment and respect for the time required by each process continue to shape the character of their products.</p>
HTML,
    'tolecarnes' => <<<'HTML'
<p>The story of <strong>Tolecarnes</strong> has been linked to cattle farming since <strong>1960</strong>. Three generations have been dedicated to raising cattle in the Montes de Toledo, an experience passed from one generation to the next that continues to shape the way they understand livestock farming.</p>
<p>Their farm is located on a dehesa on the northern side of the <strong>Montes de Toledo</strong>, an environment particularly well suited to the natural growth of pasture. For much of the year, the animals graze freely and make direct use of the resources provided by the land, maintaining a way of farming that is closely connected to the territory.</p>
<p>Their diet is adapted to the conditions of each season. During the months of greatest abundance, some of the pasture is harvested and stored for winter and, when the animals' diet needs to be supplemented, they use <strong>100% natural feed made exclusively from plant-based ingredients</strong>. This feed is produced at the Menasalbas cooperative of which they are members, a connection that allows them to <strong>follow its production closely and maintain greater control over the feeding of their cattle</strong>.</p>
<p>Over the years, they have combined this traditional approach to farming with livestock selection that takes genetics into account, as well as everything surrounding the animal throughout its life. The environment, diet and activity all form part of the same process aimed at producing balanced, high-quality meat.</p>
<p>The experience accumulated over three generations has allowed them to seek greater consistency in their meat, paying particular attention to characteristics such as <strong>tenderness, juiciness and marbling</strong>, without losing sight of the flavour that is distinctive to each cut.</p>
<p>Today, Tolecarnes continues its livestock farming activity in the Montes de Toledo, maintaining an approach in which the dehesa, the animals' diet and knowledge passed down through generations remain the foundation of its meat.</p>
HTML,
    'puente-robles' => <<<'HTML'
<p>The story of <strong>Puente Robles</strong> begins long before the brand itself existed. As a child, Cesáreo would already accompany his father to the market to sell the pigs the family had raised and cared for throughout the year. That connection with livestock farming and local produce would eventually become, in the 1980s, the family project he started with Carmen and which now has <strong>more than 30 years of experience</strong>.</p>
<p>From <strong>Fermoselle, in the heart of the Arribes del Duero area</strong>, that story remains closely tied to the territory. They work with small livestock farmers from the Arribes and the Salamanca dehesa, maintaining a close relationship with raw materials whose origin forms part of the character of their hams, cured meats and other traditional products.</p>
<p>Their production retains many of the working methods traditionally used in the area. In Fermoselle they have their own cutting facilities and drying rooms, where hams and cured meats develop slowly, making use of the conditions found in the Arribes del Duero. Tradition remains a fundamental part of the process while the facilities have also been adapted to meet today's requirements.</p>
<p>That connection with Fermoselle can also be seen in the people who are part of the project. Alongside various members of the family itself, professionals from the local area work there, giving continuity to an activity that for decades has been linked to the production of Iberian products and the traditional flavours of this part of Zamora.</p>
<p>Among their products, <strong>acorn-fed cular chorizo</strong> has become one of the most widely recognised. It was named best Iberian chorizo at the Covaleda Artisan Chorizo Fair in 2023 and later received a <strong>Gold Medal at the Concours International de Lyon 2025</strong> and a Silver Medal in the 2026 edition.</p>
<p>Today, Puente Robles maintains the same connection between family, territory and production with which its journey began. From Fermoselle, it continues to work alongside livestock farmers from the surrounding area and to preserve a way of making its products in which the origin of the raw material, traditional recipes and slow curing continue to shape their character.</p>
HTML,
    'el-catedratico' => <<<'HTML'
<p>The story of <strong>El Catedrático</strong> grew out of a family experience connected with Iberian products for <strong>three generations</strong>. Knowledge passed down over time is now reflected in a way of working that places particular emphasis on selecting each piece, traditional production methods and respect for curing times.</p>
<p>The acorn-fed Iberian pig plays a central role in their products. Their 100% Iberian acorn-fed hams and shoulders come from animals raised free-range in the dehesa, where they make use of the natural resources around them and complete their fattening during the <em>montanera</em>.</p>
<p>From there begins a process in which time plays a leading role. Their hams undergo a slow maturation that can last around <strong>36 months</strong>, allowing each piece to develop gradually until it reaches the right stage of curing.</p>
<p>Before being offered for sale, each ham is checked using the traditional <strong>calado</strong> technique, which makes it possible to assess its development and determine whether the piece is ready. It is a part of the craft that depends both on the process followed and on the experience built up over years of working with Iberian products.</p>
<p>The same care is applied to their cured meats, where they maintain traditional production methods and allow each product to develop its texture, aromas and flavour slowly. The results of this work have also received recognition outside the company itself, including the <strong>Gold award at the Frankfurt International Trophy 2025</strong> won by their 100% Iberian acorn-fed salchichón.</p>
<p>Three generations later, El Catedrático continues to work with Iberian products through an approach in which experience, the selection of raw materials and respect for time remain fundamental to the character of its products.</p>
